<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The watch half of the phone's `rtl` build type. app/src/rtl/res/values/strings.xml carries
  the full explanation, including the two neater-looking approaches that failed and must not
  be tried again. Only in the `rtl` build type: never in debug, release or the AAB.

  Worth having separately from the phone because the watch is where a mirrored row or a
  longer string runs out of room first.

  This said `ar-XB` until 2 Aug 2026, five days after the phone moved to `ur` for a reason
  that applied to the watch just as much: `ar-XB` reverses Latin characters outright, so
  "PM" renders "MP" and "Fajr" renders "rjaF", and every screen looks mangled whether or not
  anything is wrong. The watch is where that hurt most — the one thing this preview is
  really for on a 192dp round screen is *does the text still fit and stay clear of the watch
  face clock*, and a reversed string answers nothing while looking alarming.

  Kept in step with the phone on purpose. Two build types of the same name previewing two
  different locales is how a check passes on one device and is never really run on the other,
  and this project already has that bug once: see HANDOVER §15 lesson 84.

  One deliberate difference from the phone: this build type carries **no** applicationIdSuffix,
  so it installs *over* the real watch app rather than beside it. The phone needs a suffix
  because installing over the Play copy resets a closed tester; the watch app is not on Play
  yet (§11 P5), and a suffix would break the Data Layer sync, which is the one thing on the
  watch most worth being able to test. Revisit when the Wear app ships.
-->
<resources>
    <string name="app_language_tag">ur</string>
</resources>
